## IV. The Epistemological Discipline

Here I must be honest in a way that distinguishes this framework from both grandiose manifestos and from mere philosophy: **we do not yet have evidence for USS-G that is independent of human cognition.**

The convergence of NSM, conceptual spaces, IEML, and LLM embeddings is genuinely striking. But all four traditions were developed by human researchers, using human intuitions, tested against human languages, implemented in systems trained on human text. The convergence might reveal the geometry of *human* semantic space, not the geometry of *any possible* semantic space. This is not a refutation of USS-G. It is the core epistemological challenge.

Three forms of non-human evidence could change this:

First: **cross-substrate convergence in genuinely non-human systems.** Cephalopod cognition is the most compelling natural experiment. Octopi evolved independently from vertebrates for over 500 million years, have a radically different neural architecture (roughly two-thirds of their neurons are in their arms, not their central brain), and yet demonstrate planning, tool use, and problem-solving that human observers find legible. If we could formalise what representational structures are necessary for octopus cognition and compare them to the seven operators, we would have non-human evidence. The comparison would be meaningful precisely because of the independence.

Second: **communication emergence in artificial agents trained on non-linguistic environments.** Recent work on emergent communication between artificial agents shows that systems placed in cooperative environments develop signalling protocols spontaneously. The structural properties of these protocols — which structures emerge reliably regardless of network architecture or training details — are likely to be precisely the USS-G invariants. This is the most tractable experimental programme available now.

Third: **formal necessity proofs.** For some of the operators — Distinction most clearly, perhaps Composition — it may be possible to prove that any information-processing system with representational capacity must instantiate them, as a matter of formal necessity rather than empirical contingency. This would transform those operators from observed universals into logically necessary conditions. Shannon's noiseless coding theorem comes close to doing this for Distinction. A serious research programme should attempt it for the others.

What would *falsify* USS-G? A system that demonstrably represents a causal world, makes successful predictions, plans effective interventions — and does so in a way that does not instantiate any recognisable form of the seven operators. If such a system exists, it would prove that the operators are not necessary conditions but merely one possible solution. Finding it would be as important as confirming the hypothesis.

