## V. The Embedding Space as Scientific Instrument

The telescope does not invent galaxies. It reveals structure that exists independently. But a badly calibrated telescope also introduces aberrations — false structures that are artefacts of the instrument.

The critical methodological question for LLM embedding spaces is exactly this: which geometric structures in these spaces are real semantic geometry, and which are artefacts of training on human language corpora? The fact that mT5 discovers cross-lingual semantic alignment without explicit training is genuinely impressive. But it could be because human languages all encode the same world in structurally similar ways — or because human languages all evolved under the same cognitive constraints. Neither interpretation tells us about intelligence in general.

What would a *calibrated* instrument look like? I propose: embedding spaces derived from models trained on radically different data — not different languages but different modalities entirely. A model trained only on sensorimotor trajectories. A model trained only on logical proofs. A model trained only on musical structures. If the geometric organisation of these spaces converges with language-derived embedding spaces in specific ways — specifically in the structural regions corresponding to the seven operators — that convergence is evidence that is genuinely independent of linguistic structure.

This is a tractable experiment. It has not been done.

