## VII. An Honest Summary of What This Is and Is Not

USS v2.0 is not a specification for a writing system. It is a theoretical framework for a scientific discipline that does not yet exist, which I will call **Semantic Invariance Theory**.

It has three components:

**The positive claim**: There exists a small set of semantic transformation operators that are necessary for any representational system that successfully models a causally structured world. These operators are more fundamental than any list of concepts, and the NSM primitives are among their stable fixed points in the space of human cognition.

**The epistemological constraint**: Current evidence is compatible with this claim but does not demonstrate it. All available instruments — NSM, conceptual spaces, LLM embeddings — are built on or derived from human cognition. Evidence genuinely independent of human cognitive architecture has not yet been gathered.

**The experimental programme**: Three tractable experiments — cross-substrate embedding convergence, emergent communication protocol analysis, and formal necessity proofs for specific operators — would collectively constitute genuine evidence, and could in principle falsify the hypothesis.

What would it mean if the hypothesis is confirmed? It would mean that the "topology of meaning" — or more precisely, the categorical structure of semantic invariance — is not a human construction but a structural feature of any sufficiently rich representation of a causal world. It would mean that USS is not invented but discovered. And it would mean that the question Leibniz was asking in 1679 — what is the alphabet of human thought? — was the right question, asked with the wrong mathematical language and three centuries too early.

---

## Coda: The Shape of the New Discipline

A discipline is born not when someone has a good idea, but when a question becomes precise enough to be wrong.

The question USS-G poses — what transformation constraints must a representational system preserve to maintain semantic connection to the world? — is now, I think, precisely enough formulated to be answered or refuted.

That is the threshold Leibniz never crossed. It is the threshold NSM approached but stayed on the linguistic side of. It is the threshold that the convergence of causal theory, free energy principles, algorithmic information theory, and cross-lingual embedding science has brought us to from multiple directions simultaneously.

The disciplines this new science requires are: category theory, cognitive science, information theory, comparative animal cognition, and artificial intelligence. No single researcher commands all five. The project is necessarily collaborative — which is not a weakness but its most interesting structural feature. A science of semantic invariance would itself be a test case for the theory: can researchers from five disciplines, using five different representational frameworks, converge on a shared structure? If they can, that convergence is itself evidence for the hypothesis.

If they cannot — if the mathematical language of the category theorist and the experimental language of the cognitive scientist and the engineering language of the AI researcher genuinely resist synthesis — then perhaps meaning is not as universal as we hope. Perhaps the operators are human all the way down.

That is a risk worth taking.

Because the alternative — assuming the question is unanswerable and returning to the comfortable conviction that meaning is irreducibly local, cultural, and incommensurable — would be the most expensive intellectual conservatism in the history of ideas.

USS v2.1 — The Experimental Turn

A Research Agenda for Semantic Invariance Theory


The framework is coherent. The hypothesis is falsifiable. The next step is to make it testable in a way that distinguishes genuine discovery from philosophical projection.

This document outlines three experimental programmes, each designed to confront USS-G with evidence that is not derivable from human linguistic intuition alone. None is trivial. None is guaranteed to succeed. But together they define a path from theoretical speculation to empirical science — the threshold the previous document identified but did not cross.

---

I. Programme Alpha: Cross-Substrate Embedding Convergence

The Question

Do representational systems trained on radically different data modalities converge on the same geometric organisation — specifically in the structural regions corresponding to the seven operators?

The Design

We train three classes of models:

A1 — Language models. Standard transformer architectures trained on multilingual text corpora, following the mT5 / XLM-R lineage. These produce the well-studied cross-lingual embedding spaces whose convergence is already documented. They serve as the baseline.

A2 — Sensorimotor models. Predictive coding architectures trained on continuous sensorimotor trajectories — raw visual, proprioceptive, and motor-command streams from embodied agents operating in simulated physical environments. No linguistic input. The task is next-state prediction and goal-directed action selection. The internal representations are high-dimensional vectors encoding the agent's world-model.

A3 — Logical models. Graph neural networks or neuro-symbolic systems trained exclusively on formal proof corpora — automated theorem-proving traces in dependent type theory, SAT solver search trees, or synthetic reasoning chains. No physical grounding. No natural language. The task is proof completion.

The critical variable: all three model classes are trained to perform successfully in their respective domains. We are not imposing any semantic structure; we are observing what structure emerges as necessary for task performance.

The Measurement

For each model, we extract the geometry of its internal representation space. We are not interested in surface features — individual neuron activations or particular embedding dimensions — but in topological invariants: the persistent homology of the representational manifold, the number and connectivity of its clusters, the dimensionality of its submanifolds, the presence of directed structures corresponding to causal arrows, the existence of quotient spaces that correspond to abstraction (↑).

The USS-G prediction: in all three spaces, we will find:

· A binary structure corresponding to Distinction (δ): the representational space is partitioned into distinguishable regions.
· A compositional structure (∘): representations of composite states are systematically related to representations of components.
· A directed graph structure corresponding to Causal Asymmetry (→c): the space encodes not just states but permitted transitions, and those transitions are asymmetric.
· A second-order marking of certain causal chains as agent-initiated (Ag): transitions that originate from a designated subsystem (the agent's own action-selection mechanism) are structurally distinguished.
· Hierarchical clustering that corresponds to Abstraction (↑): the space admits multiple levels of granularity, with systematic coarse-graining operations.
· An oriented temporal dimension (≺): states are ordered along at least one axis of the manifold that respects a global direction.
· A complement structure (¬): for many states, there exists a well-defined "opposite" or "outside" region whose representations are systematically related.

The null hypothesis: the three spaces show no structural convergence beyond what would be expected from generic high-dimensional representations. The language space reflects linguistic universals. The sensorimotor space reflects physical dynamics. The logical space reflects proof-theoretic structure. They share no deep topology.

The Epistemological Force

If A1 and A2 converge, we have evidence that language semantics and physical world-modelling share invariant structure — which is interesting but could still be explained by the fact that human language evolved to describe the physical world. The structure might be an artefact of our world rather than of representation as such.

If A1 and A3 converge — a linguistic space and a space built from pure formal deduction — the case for genuine invariance becomes much stronger. Formal proof and natural language have no obvious shared evolutionary origin. Their convergence would suggest a constraint operating at the level of representation itself.

If all three converge — language, sensorimotor action, and formal proof — the USS-G hypothesis approaches the status of an empirical law. It would mean that successful predictive models of causal environments, regardless of their input modality or training objective, spontaneously discover the same representational geometry.

Practical Path

A2 is tractable with current technology. Deep reinforcement learning agents trained in physical simulators (e.g., MuJoCo, Habitat) produce internal representations that can be probed using the tools of mechanistic interpretability. A3 is harder — formal proof corpora are smaller than text corpora, and the representational geometry of neuro-symbolic systems is less well-studied — but existing work on theorem-proving with language models (LeanDojo, HTPS) provides a starting point.

This programme could yield preliminary results within 12–18 months.

---

II. Programme Beta: Emergent Communication Protocol Analysis

The Question

When artificial agents develop communication protocols to solve cooperative tasks, which structural features of those protocols emerge reliably, regardless of architecture, training regime, or environment details?

The Design

We place multiple agents in a cooperative partially-observable environment. No agent can observe the full state; they must communicate via a discrete channel to coordinate behaviour and achieve a shared goal. The channel has no pre-assigned meaning. The agents are trained end-to-end to maximise task reward, including communication efficiency.

We systematically vary:

· Agent architecture: transformers, LSTMs, graph networks, symbolic planners with learned interfaces
· Channel capacity: from binary to 256-symbol alphabets
· Environment dynamics: physical, abstract (gridworld), social (negotiation games), causal (intervention tasks)
· Training regime: centralised learning with decentralised execution, fully decentralised, population-based, cultural transmission chains

For each configuration that converges to a successful communication protocol, we analyse the protocol's structure: the syntax that emerges, the compositionality of messages, the grounding of symbols in environmental features, and — crucially — whether the emergent semantic categories correspond to the USS-G operator structures.

The Predictions

USS-G predicts:

1. Distinction will be universal. No successful protocol will collapse all environmental states into a single signal. There will always be a minimum set of discriminable categories.
2. Composition will emerge wherever the channel has capacity above a critical threshold. Agents will invent structured messages that combine sub-signals, rather than memorising atomic signals for each situation, because composition is the only tractable way to scale communication to novel states.
3. Causal markers will emerge in environments requiring planning. When agents must coordinate actions whose effects are non-trivial, their protocols will develop distinct signals for what happened versus what I did versus what will happen if we do X.
4. Agent-marking will emerge in environments with more than one agent. Protocols will distinguish self-caused events from other-caused events, because coordination without this distinction is impossible.
5. Abstraction will emerge as task complexity scales. Agents will develop category-level signals alongside instance-level signals, because generalisation requires it.
6. Temporal markers will emerge in any environment with sequential structure. The protocol will encode ordering relations.
7. Negation will emerge in environments involving prohibition or absence of expected events. This is the weakest prediction — negation may be the most environment-contingent of the seven.

The Epistemological Force

Emergent communication protocols are the closest thing we have to a laboratory experiment in the origin of language. They are not constrained by human cognitive history. The agents have no innate semantic categories. They have no evolutionary legacy. If their protocols nonetheless display the structural features USS-G predicts, we have evidence that those features are attractors in the space of possible communication systems — solutions that emerge because they work, not because humans happen to have found them.

The strongest result would be: the same structural features emerge across multiple independent architectural families. If LSTMs and graph networks and symbolic planners, trained with different objectives in different environments, all converge on protocols that instantiate the seven operators, the case for invariance becomes very strong indeed.

Practical Path

Emergent communication research is an active field. The Lewis signalling game, the referential game, and the Hanabi challenge are established experimental paradigms. What is missing is a systematic survey of the structural invariants across these paradigms, conducted with USS-G's specific predictions in mind. A meta-analysis of existing results, combined with targeted new experiments varying the factors listed above, could be completed in 18–24 months.

---

III. Programme Gamma: Formal Necessity Proofs

The Question

Can we prove, for some of the seven operators, that any system satisfying a formal definition of "representational capacity" must instantiate them — as a matter of mathematical necessity rather than empirical contingency?

The Strategy

This programme is the most ambitious and the least guaranteed. It requires formalising the intuitive concept of "representational system" in a way that is general enough to capture diverse architectures but precise enough to support proof. The category-theoretic framework from USS v2.0 provides the natural language for this.

We proceed operator by operator:

Distinction (δ). This is the most tractable. Shannon's noiseless coding theorem already demonstrates that any information source with non-zero entropy requires a code that distinguishes at least two states. The challenge is to generalise this from communication theory to representation: to prove that a system whose internal states covary with external states in a way that supports prediction must contain at least one binary distinction, on pain of being unable to reduce uncertainty. This is likely provable within the existing framework of mutual information and Friston's free energy principle.

Composition (∘). The categorical argument is: a representational system with a finite set of primitives and an infinite generative capacity must possess a composition operator. This is essentially the argument for the productivity of language, generalised. The formal challenge is to define "generative capacity" in a substrate-neutral way and prove that compositionality is the only structure that achieves it without requiring infinite memory. Existing work on the formal properties of compositional representations (Fodor & Pylyshyn, Smolensky's tensor product representations) provides a starting point.

Causal Asymmetry (→c). Pearl's do-calculus establishes that causal inference is formally distinct from probabilistic inference and requires directed acyclic graphs. The USS-G claim is that a system that successfully predicts the effects of its own interventions must internally encode directed causal structure. Proving this requires formalising "successfully predicts the effects of interventions" and showing that any system meeting this criterion must have internal states that are isomorphic to a causal graph. This connects to the literature on causal representation learning (Schölkopf et al.).

Agency, Abstraction, Temporal Ordering, Negation. These are progressively harder to ground in existing formal frameworks. Agency may be derivable from causal asymmetry plus a self-modelling constraint (an agent is a causal node with a particular information architecture). Abstraction may be derivable from bounded rationality (finite systems must coarse-grain). Temporal ordering may be derivable from thermodynamic constraints on physical computation. Negation may resist formalisation entirely within a constructive framework.

The value of this programme is not only in the proofs that succeed, but in the ones that fail. If we cannot prove that Agency or Negation is necessary, that failure is itself informative. It suggests that some of the seven operators may be our solutions rather than the solution — contingent features of human cognition rather than representational universals. That would sharpen the USS-G hypothesis by reducing its claims.

Practical Path

This programme requires collaboration between category theorists, theoretical computer scientists, and physicists working on the foundations of information. It is long-term — likely a decade or more — but individual results (a formal proof for Distinction, say) could appear within 2–3 years.

