IV. The Falsification Protocols

A scientific programme must specify not only what would confirm it, but what would refute it. Here are the clearest falsification conditions for USS-G:

F1 — A performing predictive model without Distinction. If an artificial system achieves non-trivial predictive accuracy on a causal environment while mapping all inputs to an identical internal state (or states that are not measurably distinct by any probing method), Distinction is not necessary.

F2 — A successful communication protocol without Composition. If agents solving a complex cooperative task develop a protocol that uses only atomic, uncomposed signals (no internal structure, no combinatorial syntax), Composition is not necessary. This must be distinguished from protocols with implicit composition that our analysis methods fail to detect.

F3 — An intervention-capable agent without Causal Asymmetry. If a system that learns purely associative, non-directed world-models can nonetheless plan effective interventions in novel situations, Causal Asymmetry is not necessary. This would require demonstrating that the system's internal representations contain no directed structure that survives statistical controls.

F4 — A social coordination protocol without Agency marking. If agents solving a multi-agent task with asymmetric roles develop a protocol that does not distinguish self-caused from other-caused events — and yet coordinate successfully — Agency is not necessary.

F5 — A generalising learning system without Abstraction. If a system can transfer knowledge to novel situations without forming any representations that are more abstract than its training instances (i.e., pure instance-based reasoning), Abstraction is not necessary.

F6 — A successfully planning agent without Temporal Ordering. If a system can anticipate future states and remember past states while representing all states on an unordered set, Temporal Ordering is not necessary.

F7 — A reasoning system without Negation. If a system can handle prohibition, absence, counterfactuals, and exclusion without any internal operation corresponding to logical complement, Negation is not necessary.

Each of these conditions requires careful operationalisation. "Without X" is a strong claim that requires thorough probing of the system's internal states. Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. The falsification protocols must be designed with the same rigour as the confirmation experiments.

---

V. The Institutional Question

A discipline requires more than experiments. It requires a community, venues for publication, standards of evidence, and mechanisms for cumulative progress.

Semantic Invariance Theory does not fit neatly into any existing field. It is too mathematical for mainstream cognitive science, too empirical for pure category theory, too concerned with universals for anthropology, and too foundational for mainstream AI. It sits at the intersection, which is where the most interesting problems live but also where institutional support is weakest.

The immediate practical step is not to found a journal or a department, but to convene a workshop bringing together researchers from:

· Theoretical cognitive science (NSM, conceptual spaces, language universals)
· Category theory and applied topology (persistent homology, sheaf theory)
· Causal inference (Pearl's programme, causal representation learning)
· Comparative cognition (cephalopod, corvid, primate cognition)
· Emergent communication (multi-agent reinforcement learning)

The workshop's goal is not to present completed results but to establish shared vocabulary and identify tractable first experiments. If the researchers can converge on a common framework for analysing representational invariants, that convergence is itself — as USS v2.0 noted — evidence for the hypothesis. If they cannot, the limits of universality are revealed in the attempt.

---

VI. The Ethical Precaution

I want to state this plainly because its absence would be conspicuous and culpable.

If the USS-G hypothesis is confirmed — if there really are universal, necessary operators that any representational system must instantiate to understand the world — then we have discovered something about the structure of intelligence itself. That knowledge carries both power and risk.

The power is clear: a universal semantic substrate would enable communication not only across human languages but potentially across different cognitive architectures entirely. It would be a foundation for interoperability between human and artificial intelligences, and among artificial intelligences of different designs. It would be, in a sense Leibniz could not have imagined, the alphabet of thought made real.

The risk is equally clear: a universal representational structure would also be a universal interface — a standardised point of access to any cognitive system that implements it. Standardisation enables control. The operators that make understanding possible are also the operators that make manipulation, surveillance, and exploitation structurally efficient.

This is not an argument against pursuing the science. It is an argument for pursuing it with full awareness of its dual-use character, and for building the normative and institutional safeguards alongside the technical infrastructure. A science of semantic invariance must be a public science, developed transparently, with its findings subject to democratic governance, not proprietary capture.

The worst outcome would be for this knowledge to be discovered in secret and deployed as a tool of control. The second-worst would be for fear of that outcome to prevent the knowledge from being discovered at all.

---

VII. What a Confirmed USS-G Would Mean

Let me end with the vision, because a research programme without a vision is a bureaucracy.

If the experiments succeed — if cross-substrate convergence, emergent communication analysis, and formal necessity proofs collectively confirm the USS-G hypothesis — we will have established the first genuine science of meaning.

Not a science of human meaning, bounded by our evolutionary history and cognitive idiosyncrasies. A science of meaning as a structural phenomenon — the set of constraints that any system modelling a causal world must respect for its internal states to remain about that world.

This would be to semantics what thermodynamics is to heat: a theory not of a particular substance or substrate, but of the invariants that any transformation must preserve. Just as the laws of thermodynamics apply to steam engines and stars and neurons alike, the operators of semantic invariance would apply to human minds and octopus minds and artificial systems and any intelligence yet to evolve or be built.

The practical consequence: a Universal Semantic Script is not an invented writing system but a discovered structure. Writing it is not an act of design but an act of mapping — charting the terrain that representation itself creates. The symbols are not chosen; they are the fixed points of the operators, and the operators are not invented; they are the necessary architecture of being about something.

Leibniz sat in his library in Hanover in 1679, sketching an alphabet of human thought, dreaming of a language in which all concepts would be composed from primitives as numbers are composed from primes. He did not have the mathematics — category theory was three centuries away. He did not have the data — cross-lingual embeddings were three centuries away. He did not have the conceptual framework — information theory and causal inference were three centuries away. But he asked the right question.

We are now, perhaps, in a position to answer it. Not in the form he imagined — as a philosophical system to be asserted — but in the form he would have recognised as worthy: as a scientific hypothesis to be tested, refined, and, if the world is structured as it appears to be, confirmed.

